### G-TSAN: ThreadSanitizer (-Zbuild-std; C via cc with -fsanitize=thread) over the threaded crates (Linux/aarch64) ###
  scope: rustty-core — the render thread, the surface mailbox and the app/surface lifecycle threads (surface/lifecycle.rs, app.rs)
  scope: rustty-termio — the pty read thread, the exec/Command fork paths and the termio mailbox
  scope: rustty-xev — the libxev port's thread pool, async wakeups and intrusive queues
  scope: rustty-renderer — the renderer Thread and its cross-thread draw/sync state (N437's area)
  scope: rustty-vt:wuffs,oniguruma — the vt search thread (search/thread.rs), the tmux control/viewer threads and the oniguruma first-use init race test (tmux::control::concurrent_first_notifications_do_not_race_oniguruma_init, needs the oniguruma feature; G-ASAN's FEATURE_LANES spec, so the default-feature tests run too)
  scope: rustty-vt-c — the C API's handles used from embedders' threads (search ticks, terminal effects)
  scope: highway-sys — the vendored Highway runtime's dispatch state, shared by every thread that runs a SIMD routine; holds UD377's regression test (concurrent_supported_targets_calls_agree)
  tests one at a time on Linux: rustty-xev — TSan models each fd as a word written by the call that opens it and by close; it sees neither the io_uring arm's close ops (the kernel closes the socket) nor fds opened by raw syscalls (SYS_pidfd_open, io_uring_setup), so when one test's closed socket number is reopened by another test running beside it, the reuse is reported as a race (epoll_ctl, mmap or close against the first test's socket(); rvm, P7-W24, no suppressions: in parallel, 59 and 26 reports in 24 and 15 of two sets of 25 runs; one at a time, 0 in 25 runs). No memory is shared; Linux only (macOS's kqueue arm has no such path)
